import Native

-- §7.4 of `lower_forward.h`: a temporary a *call* fills, copied into where it was wanted.
--
-- `tagged` is given somewhere to build a string and the caller then copies the result into the local
-- it meant all along, so the call could have been handed that local to begin with. Redirecting it
-- moves the writes earlier, which is only sound where the callee cannot see the destination by any
-- other route.
--
-- The three functions below hold four halves of that between them, and the `.lower.expect` beside
-- this file is where to read each of them:
--
--  * `built`: `call tagged, %text, %n` - forwarded. The local is fresh where the call runs, nothing
--    else has its address, and both the temporary and the sixteen-byte copy stop existing.
--  * `built`: `call Append(String).+, %v, %text, ..` followed by `copy %text, %v, 16` - declined,
--    and by the oldest of the rules: the release of the old string stands between the call and the
--    copy, and a call in that stretch is something this pass cannot say is unable to reach the
--    destination.
--  * `inLaterBlock`: forwarded, and it is §7.4.1's widening - the destination is passed on only in
--    blocks the call's own block cannot be reached from, so the call still cannot have the address.
--  * `inLoop`: declined, and it is the clause that costs bytes rather than saving them. The append
--    is written *below* the call, which in a straight line would put it out of reach - but the block
--    is a loop body, so on the second time round it is above it.
--
-- The assertion is against the *allocator* as well as the length, because that is what a wrong
-- redirect shows up in - a call writing a destination it also reads would build the wrong bytes or
-- release a run twice, and `heapNext` is the only witness to the second. Native only, for the reason
-- `StringReclaim.yana` gives.

let &tags = 0 :: Int

fn tagged(n: Int) -> String:
    tags = tags + 1
    return "n={n}"

fn built(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &text = tagged(n)
    text = text + "!"
    return truncate(length(text)) :: Int

-- §7.4.1's widening, and `Text.showSigned`'s shape written small: the destination is only ever
-- passed on in a *later* block, and no path from that block leads back to the one holding the call,
-- so the call still cannot have the address and `tagged` writes `%text` directly.
fn inLaterBlock(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &text = tagged(n)

    if n > 0:
        text = text + "!"

    return truncate(length(text)) :: Int

-- And the half a position comparison alone gets wrong. The append is written *below* the call, which
-- in a straight line would put it out of the call's reach - but the block is a loop body, so on the
-- second time round it is above it. The block reaches itself, and this declines.
fn inLoop(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < n:
        let &text = tagged(i)
        text = text + "!"
        total = total + (truncate(length(text)) :: Int)
        i = i + 1

    return total

fn main() -> Int:
    -- Once first, so every size class these strings reach has been mapped and the free lists are
    -- warm. Everything measured after this is reuse rather than growth.
    let warm = built(7)
    let settled = heapNext

    -- The length, which is what a redirect that wrote the wrong bytes changes, and the heap pointer,
    -- which is what a redirect that lost a release changes.
    if built(7) != warm then return -1
    if !(heapNext == settled) then return -2

    -- The two blocks above, held to the same two assertions.
    if inLaterBlock(7) != 4 then return -3
    if inLaterBlock(0 - 1) != 4 then return -4
    if !(heapNext == settled) then return -5

    if inLoop(3) != 12 then return -6
    if !(heapNext == settled) then return -7

    -- And that the calls really ran rather than being folded away: two from `built`, two from
    -- `inLaterBlock` and three from `inLoop`.
    if tags != 7 then return -8

    -- "n=7!" is four units.
    return warm
